WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Modern mobile applications rely on a clear spatial hierarchy.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.400
Screens must slide, stack, and dismiss in a way that feels consistent to the user.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.400
For an iOS engineer moving to react native,

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.600
the transition doesn't require a total reconstruction of how you think about abstracture.

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.800
Both ecosystems have moved away from the imperative UI kit model,

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.600
where you manually pushed and popped view controllers in favor of a declarative navigation tree.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:35.200
We can map the core SwiftUI navigation APIs directly to their react navigation 7 equivalents.

8
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.200
In both frameworks, you describe the structure of the navigation tree

9
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.600
and allow the underlying system to manage the transitions.

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.400
Transitioning to cross-platform development doesn't mean losing the structure of a native routing architecture.

11
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.600
The logic carries over, the foundational container translates directly.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.200
In SwiftUI, you wrap content in a navigation stack.

13
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.600
In react navigation 7, the equivalent is create static navigation.

14
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.000
This static API is the current recommendation for react native when working with a fixed set of screens.

15
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.600
The shift between these frameworks is found in how you trigger a navigation change.

16
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.000
The dot append in Swift corresponds to executing navigation.navigate in JavaScript.

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Returning to a previous screen follows the same logic.

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.800
Removing the last item from a Swift array maps to the navigation.go back command.

19
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.400
To clear the stack and return to the root, you reset the navigation path in Swift

20
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.800
or fire the navigation dot reset command in react native.

21
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.600
While the trigger mechanism moves from state arrays to programmatic hooks,

22
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.000
the underlying logic flows remain the same.

23
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.600
When screens are isolated, you must pass data structures or IDs between them.

24
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:51.800
In SwiftUI, you bind a destination view to a specific data type using navigation destination 4.

25
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.000
React navigation handles this through the route.params object.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.800
The receiving screen reads from this object the same way a SwiftUI view reads its bound value.

27
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.400
React native lacks a direct equivalent to iOS delegate protocols for passing data back to a previous screen.

28
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.200
You typically use a callback function instead.

29
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.600
For native engineers, the primary concern when moving to a JavaScript framework

30
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.400
is maintaining strict compile time safety.

31
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.400
By utilizing TypeScript, React navigation 7 provides developers with safety checks

32
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.000
that mirror the Swift development experience.

33
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.200
You define a parameter list in TypeScript to lock down the expected payloads for every route in the navigator.

34
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.600
Omit a required parameter or pass the wrong type

35
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.400
and the TypeScript compiler flags it in your editor,

36
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.800
providing a failsafe matching Xcode's type checker.

37
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:47.000
Migrating to React native doesn't require abandoning the protection of a typed compiler.

38
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Beyond the data layer, we must consider how screens visually appear to the user,

39
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
such as Models and Sheets.

40
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
SwiftUI's native .sheet modifier translates to the React native stack option, FormSheet,

41
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
which renders the standard iOS sheet with swipe to dismiss.

42
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Similarly, the . fullscreen cover modifier maps to the fullscreen modal presentation option.

43
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
For complex bottom sheets with custom snappoints,

44
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.000
developers often use the GoreHome bottom sheet library to replicate the behavior of UI sheet presentation controller.

45
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
The navigation chrome is also handled differently.

46
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.000
In SwiftUI, you use the modifiers like . navigation title or . toolbar.

47
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
React navigation moves these into configuration objects.

48
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:39.000
You use screen options for global defaults and navigation.set options for dynamic per screen updates.

49
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
These configuration options allow the app to maintain native interaction patterns

50
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
and the visual style users expect on iOS.

51
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
At the architectural level, you must be able to drive navigation from outside the immediate view hierarchy.

52
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
In SwiftUI, this often means publishing navigation paths from a separate view model.

53
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
React native provides the use navigation hook.

54
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
This allows any component or custom hook to trigger routing without passing navigation props down through the tree.

55
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.000
For events that happen entirely outside the component tree, such as background push notifications,

56
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
a dedicated navigation ref provides direct access to the routing state.

57
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Authentication follows the same logic.

58
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.000
SwiftUI handles this by checking a state variable and conditionally rendering different root views.

59
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
React navigation uses the same pattern.

60
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.000
You conditionally render different navigators based on the global authentication state.

61
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.000
When the authentication status changes, the framework swaps the active navigator automatically.

62
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.000
This logic scales to standard app structures.

63
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Embedding independent stacks within a tab view, maps directly to nesting native stack navigators inside a bottom tab navigator.

64
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Deep linking is handled through a declarative linking config, which maps incoming URLs to specific screens,

65
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.000
mirroring the purpose of the . on open URL modifier in SwiftUI.

66
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000
The structural approach to building complex apps in SwiftUI remains applicable when architecting for React native.

67
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.000
React navigation 7 introduces a choice between two API styles, static and dynamic.

68
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.000
For most apps with a fixed set of screens, you should use the static API.

69
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
It provides the best TypeScript inference and simplifies the setup.

70
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.000
The dynamic API is reserved for specific cases where your screen list must change based on runtimes data.

71
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.000
This table summarizes the relationship between the two frameworks.

72
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Most core SwiftUI routing commands have a direct counterpart in React navigation 7.

73
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:50.000
By leveraging the static API and TypeScript, your experience with declarative SwiftUI navigation gives you a clear path forward when building for React native.

